{"name":"Vanilla Drop","hex":"#ffffeb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vanilla","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tropical climbing orchid of the genus Vanilla (especially Vanilla planifolia), bearing podlike fruit yielding an extract used in flavoring food or in perfumes.","The fruit or bean of the vanilla plant."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of vanilla.","Standard, plain, default, unmodified, basic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vanilla"},{"term":"drop","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small quantity of liquid, just large enough to hold its own rounded shape through surface tension, especially one that falls from a source of liquid.","A dose of liquid medicine in the form of a drop (sense 1)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of a liquid: to fall in drops or droplets. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","To fall (straight down) under the influence of gravity, like a drop of liquid."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drop"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vanilla","blocks":[{"text":"Vanilla is a spice derived from orchids of the genus Vanilla, primarily obtained from the seed pods of the flat-leaved Mexican and Central American vanilla (V. planifolia)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Vanilla_planifolia_%286998639597%29.jpg/330px-Vanilla_planifolia_%286998639597%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanilla"},{"title":"Vanilla (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color vanilla is a rich tint of off-white as well as a medium pale tint of yellow."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Vanilla_planifolia_1.jpg/330px-Vanilla_planifolia_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanilla_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum